# Workflow

## Sources of truth, in order

1. **The live Figma file** `ks-MarketingSite` (`IBO39siJYDhiCRtuLWUTW2`), read
   through the Figma MCP. Every geometry, type, color, and exposure value
   comes from a node read at build time. See `40-figma.mdc`.
2. **The code.** Values live in the token layers, data modules, and components.
   Read the code for current behavior.

Design intent is the Figma file. The record of what ships is the code and its
comments. These rules are the contract. Project files describe only current
behavior and requirements.

## Standing decisions

- Light mode only.
- Acceptance and the grid sweep run on the live pages. Dev-only sweep hooks
  mount through a `*-grid-check` wrapper that production aliases to a null component — no
  panel, no dedicated URL.
- The tick caps at 112px above a 1344 container; the 1344 design renders exact
  at every wider width and the side fields fill the gutters.

## Verification gates

Before you report a change as done:

- `npx tsc --noEmit` — zero errors. `npm run lint` — zero errors or warnings.
- Zero unused imports, variables, or dead code.
- Visual work is verified in the browser at the five anchors (384 · 576 ·
  768 · 960 · 1344) and one arbitrary mid-band width per band, scrollbar
  forced on. Report the measured evidence, not "looks right".
- Every value traces to a token, a named constant, or a data module.
- Count the client islands you added and the route JS you added; each section
  ships at most one island.

**Grid sweep — only when the lattice contract changes.** Run
`GRID_URL=<active-dev-url> npm run test:grid` when the change touches any of:

- `design-system/grid/` (engine, region, painting)
- `app/grid/` (panel logic, expectations, band helpers)
- section tick geometry that expectations assert (designed rows, region
  `gx`/`gy`/`gyb`/`gw`/`gh`, section stack order, footer drawer growth)

Skip it for copy, links, tokens that are not tick geometry, motion, widgets,
data modules, and ordinary CSS that does not change designed section heights
or exposure regions. When in doubt, prefer a focused browser check over a
full sweep. The sweep is minutes long; do not run it by default. Never use
it as a mid-build loop. If it fails, stay on the failing route; do not
restart the full suite from `/`.

A one-page lattice change runs only that page. `/` is home only:

```
GRID_URL=http://localhost:3000 GRID_ROUTE=/blog npm run test:grid
```

`GRID_ROUTE` is a comma-separated path prefix. `GRID_STATES=0` skips
interactive drives (engines, carousel, drawers) and checks rest geometry
only. A spec may add a page to the runner; it must not require a full-site
sweep for a one-page build.

**Fix the cause, never suppress.** Lint and type directives hide problems.

Use `npm run build` for deploy verification and bundle-size checks.

## The dev server

**Never start your own dev server.** The owner keeps `npm run dev` running;
inspect the active terminal and verify against that server's port. Two servers
share one `.next` directory and corrupt each other's chunks. If the active
server is not answering, say so and ask. Without `GRID_URL` the grid sweep
spawns its own `next dev` on port 4823 and hits the same conflict — always pass
the active server URL.

## Don't hallucinate

- Never invent copy, data, or content. Every string comes from a data module,
  Figma, or an explicit instruction.
- Never invent a component API. Read the component before using a prop.
- Never assume a file or export exists. Verify by reading it.
- When unclear, stop and ask. Undoing a bad assumption costs more.

## Search before you build

- Icons and marks: `design-system/icons.tsx` and `public/media/brand/`.
- Assets: `design-system/media.ts` and `public/media/`.
- Tokens: `design-system/tokens/` before writing any color, spacing, type,
  motion, or z value.
- Data helpers and route handlers: the `@keystone-sites/*` packages.
- Behaviour hooks: `data-action` contracts already wired (`open-chat`,
  `open-gallery`) before adding a new one.

The Figma MCP does not know the codebase; cross-reference its exports before
committing anything. It names assets with SHA hashes — rename to descriptive
kebab-case or delete the duplicate.

## Comments

- Comments explain *why*, never *what*. A useful comment carries a non-obvious
  reason or a constraint the code cannot express. Write in present tense.
  Record a Figma node id where a value came from a node.
- User-facing strings use literal Unicode characters. The only escapes are
  invisible significant characters (`\u00a0`, `\u200d`, `\u202f`), commented.

## Git

- **Commit only when the human asks in that turn.** Passing checks is not
  permission. Leave the tree dirty and report what is ready.
- New branches use `feature/`, `fix/`, or `chore/`. Keep an established shared
  branch name when work is already coordinated on that branch. Messages follow
  Conventional Commits with an imperative summary.
- Messages are ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English: active voice, one idea
  per sentence, 20 words or fewer per sentence, plain approved words ("start",
  not "initiate"; "use", not "utilize"), no noun clusters over three words.
- One commit = one logical unit; the smallest complete one (one primitive, one
  section, one token layer).
- `tsc` and `lint` pass before every commit. Never `git push --force` to
  `main`. PRs squash to a few logical commits.

## Never delete pages or routes

Pages, route handlers, and config files are removed only on explicit
instruction. Flag an apparently unused one with a comment. Internal refactors
that preserve observable behaviour are fine; the URL surface never disappears
silently.
